  <p class="legal__note">This English version is provided for information only. Only the French version of the general terms and conditions of sale is legally binding (<a href="/cgv">French version</a>).</p>
  <p class="legal__note">Event &amp; Safety Engineering, SAS (simplified joint-stock company) with share capital of €1,000, Paris Trade and Companies Register 951 249 937, EU VAT FR26 951 249 937, registered office: 15 rue des Halles, 75001 Paris, France, member of Groupe MS.</p>

  <h2>Preamble</h2>
  <p>Event &amp; Safety Engineering (hereinafter "ESE" or the "Provider"), a simplified joint-stock company and member of Groupe MS, is an engineering and advisory firm for the technical production teams of the events, live performance, scenography and interior architecture sectors. ESE works at the crossroads of three complementary fields: 3D design (Vectorworks, Blender, Rhinoceros), live performance business tools (ConnectCAD, Braceworks, Vision, MVR) and project management practices adapted to creative organisations.</p>
  <p>ESE publishes the proprietary SOCLE® method, a registered trademark, dedicated to the operational transformation of event design offices and creative agencies.</p>
  <p>These General Terms and Conditions of Sale (the "Terms") set out how all services provided by ESE to its professional clients are performed. They form the basis of the contractual relationship and apply to all assignments, supplemented where relevant by Specific Conditions for each line of business.</p>
  <p>In accordance with Articles L.441-1 et seq. of the French Commercial Code, these Terms are provided to any professional client upon request. They are available at all times on ese-techsolutions.com; the applicable version is the one in force on the date the Client signs the order.</p>

  <h2>Article 1, Definitions</h2>
  <p>In these Terms, capitalised terms have the following meaning:</p>
  <p><strong>"ESE" or "the Provider"</strong> means Event &amp; Safety Engineering, SAS with share capital of €1,000, registered office 15 rue des Halles, 75001 Paris, Paris Trade and Companies Register 951 249 937 (SIREN 951 249 937; VAT FR26 951 249 937; NAF code 90.02Z, support activities for live performance), member of Groupe MS.</p>
  <p><strong>"Client"</strong>: the legal entity that has placed an order with ESE in the course of its business. <strong>"Parties"</strong>: ESE and the Client. <strong>"Offer" or "Quote"</strong>: the commercial proposal issued by ESE. <strong>"Order"</strong>: the Client's express written acceptance of an Offer or Quote, forming the contract.</p>
  <p><strong>"Specific Conditions" (SC)</strong>: appendices specific to each line of business, which prevail over these Terms in the event of conflict. <strong>"Service Contract"</strong>: a specific document setting out how an assignment is performed, which prevails over the SC and these Terms.</p>
  <p><strong>"Services"</strong>: the services ordered within the five lines of business. <strong>"Deliverables"</strong>: documents, plans, notices, schematics, reports, files (Vectorworks, Blender, Rhinoceros), calculations, technical opinions and other work delivered by ESE.</p>
  <p><strong>"SOCLE® Method"</strong>: ESE's proprietary method, a trademark registered with the INPI (manuals, frameworks, materials, procedures, associated libraries). It is not software. <strong>"ESE Libraries"</strong>: proprietary libraries of business resources developed by ESE independently of the Services.</p>
  <p><strong>"Subcontractor"</strong>: any contributor (employee, freelancer, self-employed person or third-party company) engaged by ESE. <strong>"NDA"</strong>: a bilateral non-disclosure agreement. <strong>"Personal Data"</strong>: as defined in Article 4.1 of the GDPR. <strong>"Place of Performance"</strong>: the location(s) where the Services are performed.</p>

  <h2>Article 2, Purpose and lines of business</h2>
  <p>These Terms set out the general conditions under which the Services are performed. ESE operates through five lines of business, each of which may be subject to Specific Conditions (appendices 1 to 5):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Line 1, Technical direction (TD)</strong>: technical design and delivery of events, shows, installations and scenographies. Appendix SC-1.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 2, General production management &amp; stage management</strong>: on-site operational management in liaison with technical direction. Appendix SC-2.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 3, Risk management &amp; support with the authorities</strong>: risk analysis, safety files, support for organisers in their dealings with the administrative authorities. Appendix SC-3.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 4, SOCLE®</strong>: proprietary methodological advice and support, transfer of the method and licence to use it. Appendix SC-4.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 5, Cobel Event</strong>: advice on the transformation of event production organisations (organisation, processes, tools). Appendix SC-5.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A single Order may cover one or more lines of business; the corresponding Specific Conditions apply cumulatively, subject to the order of precedence (Article 3).</p>

  <h2>Article 3, Order of precedence</h2>
  <p>In the event of conflict, the following order of precedence applies, from highest to lowest: (1) specific NDA (confidentiality); (2) Service Contract; (3) Specific Conditions; (4) accepted Offer or Quote; (5) these Terms; (6) industry practice. The Client's general terms of purchase are expressly excluded unless ESE agrees in writing. For public contracts, the contract documents (CCAP, CCTP, AE) prevail for their mandatory provisions.</p>

  <h2>Article 4, Offer, quote, order</h2>
  <p>ESE prepares its Offer on the basis of the information provided by the Client, which is deemed accurate and complete under the Client's responsibility. The Offer is valid for thirty (30) days unless stated otherwise. The Order is formed by the Client's express written acceptance (signed acceptance, eIDAS electronic signature or purchase order) and entails full acceptance of these Terms and the applicable SC. Any accepted Order is firm and final, subject to Articles 8 and 19.</p>

  <h2>Article 5, Changes to the services</h2>
  <p>Any request for changes must be notified in writing. Within seven (7) working days, ESE informs the Client of the impact on scope, schedule and price, in the form of an amendment or an additional quote. The change is only carried out after the Client's prior written agreement.</p>

  <h2>Article 6, Prices</h2>
  <p>Prices are stated in euros excluding VAT and set in the Offer, Quote or Service Contract; VAT at the applicable rate is added. Prices are firm for the validity period of the Offer, and may then be revised according to the Syntec index. Any change in the VAT rate applies to Services not yet invoiced. Travel, accommodation and meal expenses are recharged at cost on presentation of receipts, unless a flat rate has been agreed. Each Quote also includes, on a separate line, a flat-rate administrative management fee: €120 excl. VAT below €3,000 excl. VAT of Services, €250 excl. VAT up to €10,000, €450 excl. VAT up to €25,000 and €650 excl. VAT above.</p>

  <h2>Article 7, Invoicing and payment</h2>
  <p><strong>Deposit.</strong> a deposit of 30% of the total Order amount excluding VAT is required on signature, payable in full on receipt and deducted from the balance; the Services start once it has been received, unless ESE agrees otherwise in writing. <strong>Schedule.</strong> the balance is invoiced according to the agreed schedule, based on the technical milestones; failing that, on final delivery. <strong>Payment terms.</strong> invoices are payable within 30 days of their date of issue (Article L.441-10 of the French Commercial Code). The Client accepts electronic invoicing.</p>
  <p><strong>Late payment.</strong> automatically and without reminder: interest at the ECB rate plus 10 percentage points; a fixed recovery fee of €40 per invoice; all sums due become immediately payable; in the event of litigation, a penalty of 10% of the unpaid amount, without prejudice to lawyers' and bailiffs' fees. ESE may suspend the Services in the event of non-payment, after a formal notice that remains unanswered for eight (8) days.</p>

  <h2>Article 8, Cancellation by the Client</h2>
  <p>If the Client cancels the Order (other than termination for breach), the Client must pay for the Services already performed and the costs incurred, plus compensation on the unperformed balance: more than 90 days before the main performance, 25%; between 30 and 90 days, 50%; less than 30 days, 100%. The deposit paid is retained by ESE and set off against the sums due. Cancellation must be notified by registered letter with acknowledgement of receipt or by email with read receipt.</p>

  <h2>Article 9, Performance and deadlines</h2>
  <p>ESE performs its Services with full professional independence, without any relationship of subordination. Unless stated otherwise, ESE is bound by an obligation of means (Article 1231-1 of the French Civil Code), not of result. Meeting deadlines depends on the Client providing the necessary information and access, whose accuracy it guarantees. Deadlines are indicative. In the event of a delay attributable to ESE, ESE proposes a catch-up schedule. ESE may refuse to perform a Service that breaches regulations (public building safety, labour law, etc.), or when asked to sign a technical document without the necessary information; such refusal gives rise to no compensation.</p>

  <h2>Article 10, Subcontracting</h2>
  <p>ESE is expressly authorised to use Subcontractors without the Client's prior approval, this being inherent to its business model. ESE guarantees an equivalent level of expertise and remains solely responsible for the proper performance of subcontracted Services. Every Subcontractor is bound by a confidentiality obligation at least equivalent to Article 15. For public contracts, the subcontracting rules of the CCAP prevail.</p>

  <h2>Article 11, Staff and subcontractors</h2>
  <p>ESE staff and Subcontractors remain under the hierarchical, technical and disciplinary authority of ESE and do not take direct instructions from the Client outside the scope of the Offer. ESE certifies compliance with social, tax and reporting obligations and the checks required by Articles L.8222-1 et seq. of the French Labour Code. At the Place of Performance, contributors comply with the internal rules and applicable safety rules.</p>

  <h2>Article 12, Liability of the Parties</h2>
  <p><strong>Client as organiser.</strong> where the Order relates to an event, the Client is the organiser under French law and remains solely responsible for: administrative authorisations; equipment and the Place of Performance; compliance with health, safety and prevention rules; its obligations as an employer; live entertainment licences; and taking out appropriate insurance.</p>
  <p><strong>ESE scope.</strong> ESE's liability is strictly limited to the performance of the defined Services. Where ESE signs a technical document, its liability is limited to the content of that document, excluding subsequent operating conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Cap.</strong> in the event of proven fault, ESE's liability is capped at the amount excluding VAT paid under the Order, and in any event at €1,000,000 per claim and per insurance year. Non-consequential immaterial and indirect damage (loss of business, revenue, margin, customers or opportunity, damage to reputation, etc.) is excluded. Property entrusted to ESE and intellectual property infringement are capped at €150,000. These caps do not apply to bodily injury, gross negligence or wilful misconduct, or breach of an essential obligation. The Client must notify ESE of any claim by registered letter within 30 days of becoming aware of the harmful event.</p>

  <h2>Article 13, Insurance</h2>
  <p>Through Groupe MS, ESE holds professional liability insurance "Technical services and event management" with AXA France IARD (all damage combined: €9,000,000 per insurance year; sub-limits detailed in the policy). The certificate is provided upon written request. The Client takes out the insurance specific to its activity as organiser (organiser's liability, property damage, cancellation where applicable). Each Party obtains from its insurers a mutual waiver of recourse, within the limits of Article L.121-12 of the French Insurance Code.</p>

  <h2>Article 14, Intellectual property</h2>
  <p><strong>Prior rights.</strong> each Party retains its prior rights: for ESE, the SOCLE® Method, the ESE Libraries and its proprietary know-how; for the Client, its trademarks, creative content and documents provided.</p>
  <p><strong>SOCLE® Method.</strong> the exclusive property of ESE, a trademark registered with the INPI. The Client may not register it, reproduce it, modify it or create derivative works outside an express licence, or use it for its own services to third parties. Any infringement gives rise to fixed damages of at least €200,000 per infringement, without prejudice to any additional loss and to infringement proceedings (Articles L.716-1 et seq. of the French Intellectual Property Code).</p>
  <p><strong>Deliverables.</strong> unless stated otherwise, final Deliverables are subject to a non-exclusive, non-transferable licence of use limited to the scope of the project, granted upon full payment of the price. The Client may not reuse them for another project, assign, sub-license or sell them. An assignment of economic rights may be negotiated separately (excluding the SOCLE® Method and ESE Libraries, which are inalienable). ESE may reuse the know-how developed and present the assignment in its references, subject to confidentiality.</p>

  <h2>Article 15, Confidentiality</h2>
  <p>During negotiations, performance and for five (5) years after completion, each Party undertakes not to disclose information obtained from the other Party. Excluded is information that is public through no fault of the recipient, already known, disclosed by a third party in good faith, or whose disclosure is required by an authority. Where an NDA exists, its provisions prevail. ESE may mention the Client's name and the general nature of the assignment in its marketing materials, unless the Client expressly objects on signature, and excluding information covered by an NDA.</p>

  <h2>Article 16, Personal data</h2>
  <p>The processing of Personal Data exchanged is governed by the GDPR and the amended French Data Protection Act of 6 January 1978. Where the Client provides Personal Data relating to its own activity, it acts as controller and ESE as processor (Article 28 of the GDPR); a data processing agreement (DPA) is concluded on signature. ESE implements the security measures of Article 32 and notifies any breach within 72 hours. GDPR rights may be exercised by post to ESE, 15 rue des Halles, 75001 Paris, or by email to <a href="mailto:contact@ese-techsolutions.com">contact@ese-techsolutions.com</a>.</p>

  <h2>Article 17, Image rights and publication</h2>
  <p>The Client authorises ESE to use, for reference purposes, the images, photographs, videos, plans and renderings produced during the assignment, provided no individual can be identified. Where individuals can be identified, ESE only publishes with their express consent. The Client may object to any publication by express notice. For artists and performers, recording is subject to a separate agreement under the responsibility of the Client as organiser.</p>

  <h2>Article 18, Force majeure</h2>
  <p>Neither Party is liable for a failure prevented by force majeure (Article 1218 of the French Civil Code). The Party concerned gives notice within seven (7) days. If the impediment is temporary, performance is suspended; if it lasts more than three (3) months, either Party may terminate as of right, without compensation, with sums due for Services performed remaining payable to ESE.</p>

  <h2>Article 19, Termination</h2>
  <p><strong>For breach.</strong> if a failure is not remedied within 30 days of a formal notice (registered letter), the other Party may terminate as of right, without prejudice to damages. <strong>For insolvency.</strong> in the event of collective proceedings, subject to Book VI of the French Commercial Code. <strong>For convenience by the Client.</strong> governed by Article 8. Termination is notified by registered letter with acknowledgement of receipt.</p>

  <h2>Article 20, Reversibility and return</h2>
  <p>At the end of the assignment, and subject to full payment, ESE delivers the final Deliverables and provides, on request, a copy of the project data in an open format. ESE keeps a copy for its records and legal defence for the limitation period (5 years, or 10 years for signed technical documents engaging its liability). Personal Data is erased or returned in accordance with the DPA.</p>

  <h2>Article 21, Mediation and jurisdiction</h2>
  <p>These Terms are governed by French law. In the event of a dispute, the Parties undertake to attempt an amicable settlement first, then mediation before the Paris Mediation and Arbitration Centre (CMAP). Mediation is deemed to have failed if no agreement is reached within 60 days. Failing an amicable settlement, any dispute falls under the exclusive jurisdiction of the Paris Commercial Court.</p>

  <h2>Article 22, Intuitu personae</h2>
  <p>The Order is concluded intuitu personae. The Client may not assign or delegate its rights and obligations without ESE's prior written consent. In the context of a transfer, merger or contribution, ESE may transfer its rights to any successor entity, provided it informs the Client.</p>

  <h2>Article 23, Miscellaneous</h2>
  <p>If any provision is held invalid, the others remain in force. ESE's tolerance does not constitute a waiver. These Terms are drafted in French, which is the only authoritative version. Together with the Offer, the SC and, where applicable, the Service Contract and the NDA, they constitute the entire agreement between the Parties. ESE may amend these Terms; the applicable version is the one in force on the date the Order is signed, available on ese-techsolutions.com.</p>

